What is the difference between cursor and cursorless
3:26 AM
The difference between Cursor and Cursorless comes down to what they are trying to replace. Cursor replaces the entire visual editor shell (VS Code), while Cursorless replaces your physical mouse and keyboard.
Here is the direct breakdown of how they operate:

Feature	Cursor	Cursorless
What It Is	An entire AI-powered Fork of VS Code.	A specialized Extension that plugs into VS Code or Cursor.
Core Goal	Bypasses manual coding by using cloud/local AI agents to write whole files and handle heavy logic.	Bypasses the physical mouse and keyboard by mapping the code structure directly to your voice.
How You Use It	You hit shortcuts (Ctrl+K, Ctrl+L) to prompt AI models, chat with your codebase, or trigger inline agents.	You wear a headset and say precise structural words like "take fine" or "chuck air" to instantly edit parts of the code.
Under the Hood	It embeds deep LLM contexts, code indexing, and conversational frameworks right into the editor's core.	It uses an Abstract Syntax Tree (AST) parser to look at your code and slap colored hats (tokens) on every character on your screen.
Dependencies	Runs completely standalone as its own system application.	Requires an editor (like VS Code or Cursor) plus a voice engine like Talon running on your system to process audio.


How They Fit Together In A Setup

They don't compete; they actually stack perfectly.
You can install the Cursorless extension straight into Cursor (since Cursor supports all VS Code extensions). When you do this:
1. You use Cursorless to navigate around the screen, select functions, and manipulate blocks of text using your voice via Talon.
2. You use Cursor (and its integrated agents like Cline) to handle the brutal heavy lifting, code generation, and project-wide debugging logs without doing any mechanical typing.
