What is the how much RAM does it usually take to run to the cursor and can I run it on a Ubuntu 22 server
3:26 AM

RAM Requirements for Cursor

By itself, Cursor typically uses between 500MB and 1.5GB of RAM just to start up and sit idle. However, because it is an Electron-based application (built on the VS Code core) with integrated AI indexing features, its actual memory footprint scales heavily based on what you are doing:
• Idle / Small Files: ~500MB - 1GB RAM.
• Active Project Indexing: Up to 2GB - 4GB RAM. When you open a repository, Cursor builds a local vector index of your entire codebase so the AI has context.
• Running Heavy Extensions / Agents: 4GB+ RAM. If you stack intensive extensions like Cline or run local language servers, the memory usage will spike significantly.

Running Cursor on an Ubuntu 22.04 Server

Yes, you can run Cursor on an Ubuntu 22.04 Server, but how you set it up depends on whether your server has a graphical interface (GUI).
Because Cursor is a visual desktop application, it cannot run directly inside a headless text-only terminal (TTY).

Scenario A: Your Server Has a Desktop GUI (X11 / Openbox / LXDE)

If your Ubuntu 22.04 server has a lightweight desktop environment configured (such as Openbox or LXDE), you can run Cursor directly on the hardware:
1. Download the official Linux AppImage or .deb package.
2. Launch it within your X11 graphical session.
Note: Since your environment has 4GB of physical RAM, running a full desktop environment alongside Cursor and heavy agent extensions will push your memory limits. Utilizing aggressive zram configuration (e.g., a 3:1 compression ratio with swappiness tuned to 60) will be absolutely critical to prevent the Linux Out-Of-Memory (OOM) killer from dropping your compiler loops or editor process.

Scenario B: Headless Remote Server (The Smart Workflow)

If your server is running headless (command line only) to save every megabyte of RAM for heavy compiling and static toolchains, you should not install the full Cursor desktop app on the server. Instead, use the Remote-SSH / Tunnel pattern:
1. Run the lightweight Cursor Server component or a VS Code Tunnel natively on your Ubuntu 22.04 machine. It runs headlessly and uses very little RAM (~150MB - 300MB).
2. Connect to it remotely from another device using the Cursor desktop client or via an isolated network mesh.
3. This keeps your server's physical memory completely clear to handle static toolchain compilation passes, while you keep full access to your environment.
