Since your Ubuntu 22.04 Server is running directly on the ASUS Vivobook Go 14/15 hardware (installed on that internal NVMe drive) and you are using Openbox with X11 locally on that laptop screen, you have exactly what you need to run the full client setup.
However, because that specific Vivobook Go configuration is locked to 4GB of physical DDR5 RAM, resources are incredibly tight. Running an Electron-based app like Cursor side-by-side with heavy automated extensions like Cline will immediately choke your system if it isn't strictly optimized.
Here is the exact battle plan to get Cursor running on that laptop server without crashing your environment or running out of memory.

Step 1: Maximize Your 4GB System Resources (Mandatory)

Before launching Cursor on a 4GB machine, your zram and swappiness must be configured perfectly to prevent the Linux kernel's Out-Of-Memory (OOM) killer from instantly snapping your editor process.
Since your system runs on 4GB of physical RAM, you need to ensure your 3:1 ZSTD zram compression is active to give you a 12GB virtual buffer, and your swappiness must be set to 60. If swappiness is left at the stock 100, your screen will experience severe stuttering and lag under heavy compiling loops.
Run this quick check in your terminal to verify your compression budget before launching heavy binaries:
bash
swapon -s
cat /proc/sys/vm/swappiness
Use code with caution.

Step 2: Download the Raw Standalone AppImage

Because you want to avoid container sandboxing or traditional package bloat that restricts inter-application communication, download the raw Linux AppImage binary straight to your execution folder.
Open your Openbox terminal panel and execute this consolidated text block as raw lines to fetch and prep the application:
bash
cd ~/Downloads
wget -O cursor.appimage "https://cursor.sh"
chmod +x cursor.appimage
Use code with caution.

Step 3: Run Cursor Globally on Openbox with the Sandbox Bypass

Because Cursor uses a chromium-based Electron engine, running it directly as root or within highly stripped down X11/Openbox window managers sometimes throws a sandbox initialization error.
To execute it smoothly from your command line or link it directly to your Openbox menu scripts without crashes, pass the explicit terminal flag line:
bash
./cursor.appimage --no-sandbox
Use code with caution.

💡 Critical Optimization Rule for Your Setup

To keep Cursor from devouring your 4GB of RAM while Cline is running workflows, you must disable codebase indexing the absolute moment you open the editor:
1. Open Cursor Settings (Ctrl + ,).
2. Go to Features -> Codebase Indexing.
3. Turn Index new folders automatically completely OFF.
Why this matters: If Cursor tries to spin up its background vector database thread to scan your thousands of lines of code or deep repository directories, it will consume your entire physical memory pool and freeze your X11 session. Let Cline or your static binaries handle the explicit content extraction tasks on a need-to-know basis instead.
