Since you also have LXDE installed on top of your Ubuntu 22.04 server setup, you have an excellent advantage for managing your tight 4GB RAM budget. LXDE provides a fully functional, lightweight panel (lxpanel) and desktop architecture while still keeping your memory footprint incredibly minimal—especially when it's driving the same underlying Openbox window manager you've been customizing.
Because you have LXDE ready to handle the desktop environment layout on your VivoBook Go, running Cursor becomes even cleaner. Here is how to configure it to execute perfectly from your system.

Step 1: Create a Global System Launcher

Instead of hunting for the AppImage file in your terminal every time, you can drop a standard desktop entry file straight into your local applications folder. This allows the LXDE desktop application menu (lxpanel menu) to pick up Cursor instantly, letting you launch it with a simple shortcut or menu click.
Execute this single, consolidated command block in your terminal to create the global launcher configuration:
bash
mkdir -p ~/.local/share/applications
cat << 'EOF' > ~/.local/share/applications/cursor.desktop
[Desktop Entry]
Name=Cursor
Exec=/home/slm015/Downloads/cursor.appimage --no-sandbox
Icon=system-run
Type=Application
Terminal=false
Categories=Development;IDE;
EOF
Use code with caution.
(Note: If you have your cursor.appimage saved in a different location than your Downloads directory, just adjust the path line in the Exec= row above to match your setup.)

Step 2: Refresh and Launch via LXDE

Once the desktop file is in place, you don't even need to keep a terminal open to run the editor.
1. Open your LXDE Application Menu (usually the bottom-left icon on your desktop panel).
2. Look under the Development or Other category.
3. Click Cursor to execute the binary cleanly.
By using the --no-sandbox flag built directly into that desktop entry launcher, the chromium engine underneath Cursor will bypass the strict isolation restrictions that normally cause crashes inside custom X11/Openbox desktop configurations.

