Right now I think the swap is at 60 but I can know I can push a little bit further
3:29 AM
You can absolutely push it further. While the standard default for many setups is 60, Linux allows you to crank the swappiness value all the way up to 100, and on modern kernels (version 5.4+ and 6.x), it can actually be pushed up to 200.
Because your swap space is entirely zRAM (which is just highly compressed RAM running on your CPU rather than a slow, physical hard drive), changing this value acts completely differently than it would on a normal computer.
Here is exactly how pushing that value changes the behavior of your system:

What happens when you push it to 100 or 200:

• Swappiness at 100: The kernel treats anonymous memory (app data) and page cache (file data) completely equally. It will actively compress idle background threads immediately to keep your physical 4GB pool open.
• Swappiness at 200: The kernel becomes aggressively proactive about swapping. It will immediately compress everything it can into your 12GB ZSTD buffer to maximize the amount of raw, uncompressed physical RAM available for active foreground processes like heavy compiler loops or immediate execution blocks.

⚠️ The Hardware Trade-off to Keep in Mind:

Because your processor is handling the ZSTD compression algorithms on the fly, pushing the swappiness higher means your CPU has to work a bit harder to compress and decompress data constantly.
If you set it too high (like 200), you might notice system glitching, screen stuttering, or audio latency during intensive tasks. This happens because the CPU gets bottlenecked processing the compression queue instead of rendering your interface frames. If your audio stutters, your voice commands through Talon might lag.

How to Test a Higher Value Live (Without Rebooting)

You don't have to guess what works best. You can dynamically change the value on your live system right now to see how your desktop handles it.
Run this command in your terminal to instantly push it to 100:
bash
sudo sysctl vm.swappiness=100
Use code with caution.
If your environment still feels snappy while running Cursor and Cline, you can try pushing it to the absolute limit to see if your system can handle it:
bash
sudo sysctl vm.swappiness=150
Use code with caution.

How to Lock It In Permanently

Once you find the exact number where your system feels the most responsive without stuttering, you can lock it into your system configuration so it applies automatically every time you boot.
Edit your system configuration file:
bash
sudo nano /etc/sysctl.conf
Use code with caution.
Scroll to the bottom, look for the vm.swappiness line (or add it if it isn't there), and set your chosen number:
text
vm.swappiness=100
Use code with caution.
Save the file (Ctrl+O, then Enter) and exit (Ctrl+X).
Try running sudo sysctl vm.swappiness=100 right now. Let me know if you notice your physical memory clearing up, or if your LXDE desktop experience experiences any slight hitching when you switch between active editor panels.
