The exact reason your VivoBook Go is slowing down to a crawl—even when you aren't actively doing anything in the editor—is a known architectural trap with stock VS Code / Cursor and heavy extensions like Cline and Kilo Code on a 4GB hardware base.
Here is the plain technical truth of what is happening under the hood and how to stop it:

1. The Electron + Node Fork RAM Leak

VS Code and Cursor are built on Electron (Chromium). When they sit open, they don't just run one process. Cline and Kilo Code spawn their own isolated background Node.js worker processes. Even if you aren't sending prompts:
• Kilo Code and Cline are constantly running hidden background file watchers to track changes in your workspace.
• They aggressively scan your workspace directories to build internal dynamic memory maps.
• On 4GB of physical RAM, these background scans instantly fill your uncompressed physical pool, forcing the kernel to constantly push data into your zRAM and compress it on the fly. That background compression queue is what's pinning your CPU and causing the heavy desktop slowdown.

2. Radical Workaround: Stop the Scans Live

You can instantly kill the background resource theft without closing your editor by forcing VS Code/Cursor to ignore your directories for indexing and file watching.
Open your settings configuration (Ctrl + ,), switch to the JSON view (click the small file icon in the top right corner), and paste these exact line parameters into your block:
json
"files.watcherExclude": {
  "**/.git/objects/**": true,
  "**/.git/subtree-cache/**": true,
  "**/node_modules/*/**": true,
  "**/.hg/store/**": true
},
"search.useIgnoreFiles": true,
"search.followSymlinks": false
Use code with caution.

