To understand how these fit into your system, you have to look at AI models by their functional architecture. Models are classified into distinct groups based on what kind of data they process (inputs/outputs) and how they process it.
Here are the primary classes of AI models broken down by how they operate:

1. The Autonomous / Agentic Class (The Execution Engines)

These are not just chat boxes; they are designed to act. Instead of just returning text, they analyze a goal, break it down into steps, and use external software tools to change your system state.
• How they work: They utilize structured formatting (like JSON blocks) to interact with API hooks, shell environments, and file systems.
• Examples in your stack: Cline acts as an autonomous agent shell. Under the hood, models like Gemini 2.5 Flash or Claude 3.5 Sonnet are fine-tuned for this class because they can accurately translate a prompt into system executions (like writing a file or running a terminal command) without losing track of the loop.

2. The Reasoning Class (The Code & Logic Architectures)

These models are built for deep mathematical execution, framework building, and low-level troubleshooting.
• How they work: They use an internal "Chain of Thought" buffer. They execute silent logical passes to check their own work, identify compilation errors before outputting code, and correct their architecture tracking paths.
• Examples: DeepSeek-R1, OpenAI o1/o3, or highly dense open-weights configurations like Llama-3.3-70B. They take longer to respond because they are calculating logic steps, making them perfect for kernel code debugging or static binary compilation problems.

3. The Text / Generative Class (The Fast Predictors)

These are standard Large Language Models (LLMs) optimized for speed, pattern recognition, and inline text transformations.
• How they work: They process input data arrays and instantly predict the most probable next token without running internal reasoning loops.
• Examples: Qwen 2.5 Coder or Llama 3.1 8B. Inside your workspace profile, this is exactly what Kilo Code uses to handle rapid, lag-free inline autocomplete lines while you type or pass basic dictation strings.

4. The Vision & Image Class (The Diffusers / Multimodal Engines)

This class handles spatial layout parameters, pixel arrays, and visual composition. It is split into two directions:
• Image Generation (Diffusion/Flow Match): Models like Flux.1 or Stable Diffusion take text prompts and generate raw pixel matrices from scratch. You use these when designing physical layouts, graphics, or asset matrices.
• Vision Input (Multimodal LLMs): Models that can "see" images to understand code structure. For example, you can pass a screenshot of a broken UI or a circuit schematic directly into Gemini 2.5 Flash, and the model parses the visual pixels to tell you exactly which line of code or build layer is broken.

5. The Embedding Class (The Memory Vectors)

These are highly specialized background models that don't output text or images at all.
• How they work: They translate raw text files, directories, and codebases into long strings of numbers (vectors) representing semantic meaning.
• Why they matter: This is what apps like Cursor use for local codebase indexing. It converts your workspace into a database so the autonomous agent can instantly pull relevant file context into its active memory buffer.
