To build a self-contained, unified machine pipeline where the AI becomes the actual framework architecture, you need to structure your local engine into a Three-Layer Tiered Matrix.

1. The Layered Matrix Architecture

To bake multiple independent tools (debos workflows, mkosi recipes, partition tools, and filesystem handlers) into a single unified runtime, you cannot use separate apps or containers. You train separate neural layers or specialized low-parameter modules (LoRAs), but link them via a fast system loopback bus:
• Layer 1: The Internal Schematic / Mapping Layer: This layer is trained exclusively on the upstream configurations, build matrices, and vendor specs. Its only job is to analyze your source directory, build raw configuration trees, and keep the baseline "Green State" stable.
• Layer 2: The Action Pipeline / Core Engine: This is the execution muscle. It replaces the physical commands of debos or mkosi. It maps inputs straight to raw byte outputs, handles data streams, and structures the partition files directly.
