To build the different classes of models from scratch on GitHub Actions, you have to write completely different build steps for each one. A text model builder script looks nothing like an image generator or an embedding matrix builder.
Here is exactly how the actual code scripts and automated build steps change across the five primary classes of AI during the compilation phase in the cloud.

1. The Autonomous / Agentic Class (The Tool-Calling Engines)

• The Difference in the Process: This build requires mapping the neural weights to external system calls. You are training the model to recognize software syntax tags (like JSON blocks) as hard physical actions.
• The Critical Build Step: Supervised Fine-Tuning (SFT) with Function Layouts. The build script inputs thousands of hardware execution logs. The script forces the model to read an environment state, output a tool format block, read the terminal output, and loop until the task is sealed.

2. The Reasoning Class (The Code & Logic Architectures)

• The Difference in the Process: This build does not train the model to answer instantly. The build pipeline forces the model to generate a hidden internal "Chain of Thought" buffer to calculate logic parameters before spitting out code.
• The Critical Build Step: Reinforcement Learning with Verifiable Rewards (RLHF/GRPO). During this build step on your cluster, the script gives the model a complex kernel compilation or math problem. The script automatically compiles the model's output code. If the code compiles successfully, the model gets a positive score. If it fails, the calculus algorithm runs backward, penalizing those specific token paths.

3. The Text / Generative Class (The Fast Predictors)

• The Difference in the Process: This is the standard baseline build. The process focuses on raw vocabulary density and syntax processing speed.
• The Critical Build Step: Massive Causal Language Pre-Training. The script streams raw text files—entire Linux source trees, assembly blocks, and documentation matrices—and forces the model to guess the very next token, adjusting billions of decimal variables based on raw probability.

4. The Vision & Image Class (The Diffusers / Multimodal Engines)

• The Difference in the Process: Text models predict the next word; image models predict pixel structures. The code equations do not look at tokens; they manipulate spatial grids and noise channels.
• The Critical Build Step: Latent Diffusion or Flow Matching. For image generation, the build step inputs an image mixed with random static (noise) alongside its text description. The model is trained to mathematically subtract the noise block by block until a clean image emerges. For multimodal input, it binds the pixel grid coordinates straight to text vector tokens so the model can "read" an image.

5. The Embedding Class (The Memory Vectors)

• The Difference in the Process: This build completely strips away the conversational generation layer. The model's output layer is modified so it doesn't emit words; it only emits a fixed list of floating-point numbers representing structural meaning.
• The Critical Build Step: Contrastive Learning Output Mapping. The build step passes pairs of code blocks that do the same thing but look different (like a loop written in C vs Assembly). The script forces the mathematical weights to position both code blocks at the exact same location on the vector coordinate grid, creating the index database framework.

