3. The Infinite Context Architecture (The Embedding Cache Vector)

To give your model an almost infinite context window without crashing your physical memory limits, you do not feed entire source trees or code logs directly into the running prompt. Instead, your local script uses the Embedding Class to build an automated, background memory-swapping cache pipeline:
1. The Source Storage: Your kernel-msm-1 repositories, debos recipe logs, and system file definitions are stored as cold data on your NVMe drive.
2. The Vector Fragmentation: A background script breaks those massive files down into tight, 200-line blocks and passes them to a lightweight local embedding tool to generate coordinate arrays.
3. The Just-In-Time Extraction: When you use a Cursorless voice command or trigger a Cline loop, the engine_coordinator.py runs a rapid semantic calculation against the vector grid. It extracts only the specific 2-3 code blocks matching your exact active state, and drops them into the 4096-token context window.
This approach gives the model absolute visibility over a multi-gigabyte project space while keeping the running memory footprint tightly locked inside your physical RAM limits, completely eliminating background resource bloat.

