You are mapping out a high-performance Pipeline Parallelism / Double-Buffered Stream architecture. Adapting the core logic of AirLLM—which focuses on loading and executing massive models layer-by-layer on restricted hardware—and turning it into a multi-unit pipeline completely changes how you utilize your 4GB physical RAM and 12GB ZSTD zram configuration.
Instead of running one heavy process that blocks your system while waiting for generation, you segment your execution paths into a 4-Unit Logical Pipeline (1, 2, 3, 4) across separate background threads or local server instances.
The exact data flow layout, resource optimization mechanics, and implementation scripts to build this parallel pipeline from scratch are detailed below.

1. The 4-Unit Logical Pipeline Architecture

By splitting the workload into dedicated data preparers and core execution units, you build a continuous fluid conveyor belt for tokens. Units 1 and 2 operate as Pipeline A, while Units 3 and 4 operate as Pipeline B.
• Unit 1 (Data Prep A): Actively ingests input text parameters, formats the system prompt templates, handles your background embedding cache lookups, and prepares token arrays.
• Unit 2 (Execution Core A): Receives the prepared token matrix from Unit 1, runs the deterministic inference math (temperature=0.0), and outputs the target raw code parameters.
• Unit 3 (Data Prep B): Sits completely awake and decoupled from Unit 2. It tracks the upcoming codebase fragments, reads live terminal telemetry logs, and gets the next input block prepped.
• Unit 4 (Execution Core B): Stays ready. The moment Unit 2 finishes its execution pass, Unit 3 feeds Unit 4, and Unit 4 executes the next multi-agent block immediately.

2. Streamlined Multi-Node Script Implementation

To build this yourself without heavy third-party framework overhead, you write a Python pipeline harness that creates non-blocking network or socket channels (localhost loopbacks) between four distinct lightweight process worker loops.
Create the master coordinator script (~/anyrealm-brain/core/pipeline_matrix.py):
python
#!/usr/bin/env python3
import sys
import os
import queue
import threading
import time

# 1. Thread-safe communication buffers between logical units
Queue_1_to_2 = queue.Queue(maxsize=2)
Queue_3_to_4 = queue.Queue(maxsize=2)

# Global flag to track which execution unit has the active processing lane focus
active_execution_lane = 1

def unit_1_data_prep_A():
    """
    Unit 1: Data Preparation Lane A. Ingests and tokenizes files/recipes.
    """
    print("[Unit 1] Initialized and watching input channel A.")
    while True:
        # Simulate catching an input payload from your Cline/Cursorless layer
        # In a real run, this hooks onto a local Unix socket listener
        time.sleep(0.5) # Non-blocking loopback pause
        
        # When an input arrives, wrap it with the strict debos/mkosi layout parameters
        mock_task = {"type": "debos_recipe_compile", "payload": "Extract product partition trees"}
        
        print("[Unit 1] Context parsed. Passing token data array down to Unit 2.")
        Queue_1_to_2.put(mock_task)
        time.sleep(5) # Prevent artificial spam loops

def unit_2_execution_core_A():
    """
    Unit 2: Inference Execution Engine A. Executes matrix calculations.
    """
    global active_execution_lane
    print("[Unit 2] Core A loaded into memory matrix.")
    while True:
        # Blocks cleanly until Unit 1 passes data down the pipeline
        task_data = Queue_1_to_2.get()
        
        # Wait until it is this pipeline's turn to execute
        while active_execution_lane != 1:
            time.sleep(0.01)
            
        print(f"[Unit 2] Running deterministic generation loop for: {task_data['payload']}")
        time.sleep(2) # Simulate raw token generation pass
        print("[Unit 2] Execution pass sealed. Handoff context to Pipeline B.")
        
        # Dynamic switch: Turn lane focus over to Pipeline B (Units 3 & 4)
        active_execution_lane = 2
        Queue_1_to_2.task_done()

def unit_3_data_prep_B():
    """
    Unit 3: Data Preparation Lane B. Prepares the next payload in parallel.
    """
    print("[Unit 3] Initialized and staying ready for predictive input loops.")
    while True:
        # While Unit 2 is burning math cycles, Unit 3 is already reading the next files
        # It handles your terminal error logs or upcoming mkosi declarative trees
        time.sleep(0.5)
        
        mock_next_task = {"type": "mkosi_seal_image", "payload": "Seal target systemd images"}
        
        print("[Unit 3] Input staged. Passing parameters down to Unit 4.")
        Queue_3_to_4.put(mock_next_task)
        time.sleep(5)

def unit_4_execution_core_B():
    """
    Unit 4: Inference Execution Engine B. Seala the output loops.
    """
    global active_execution_lane
    print("[Unit 4] Core B loaded into memory matrix.")
    while True:
        task_data = Queue_3_to_4.get()
        
        while active_execution_lane != 2:
            time.sleep(0.01)
            
        print(f"[Unit 4] Running deterministic generation loop for: {task_data['payload']}")
        time.sleep(2)
        print("[Unit 4] Execution pass sealed. Resetting lane focus back to Pipeline A.")
        
        active_execution_lane = 1
        Queue_3_to_4.task_done()

if __name__ == "__main__":
    print("[CORE] Spawning 4-Unit Parallel Processing Matrix...")
    
    # Initialize all four logical units as concurrent system threads
    t1 = threading.Thread(target=unit_1_data_prep_A, daemon=True)
    t2 = threading.Thread(target=unit_2_execution_core_A, daemon=True)
    t3 = threading.Thread(target=unit_3_data_prep_B, daemon=True)
    t4 = threading.Thread(target=unit_4_execution_core_B, daemon=True)
    
    t1.start()
    t2.start()
    t3.start()
    t4.start()
    
    # Keep the master runtime bus awake
    try:
        while True:
            time.sleep(1)
    except KeyboardInterrupt:
        print("\n[CORE] Pipeline matrix shut down cleanly.")
Use code with caution.
