Your main orchestrator script drops both tasks simultaneously like a starting gun. Units 1 and 3 run completely in parallel, crunching through the raw text recipes, scraping files, and mapping out the full game plan for their respective sides.
They don't execute a single system write command yet. Instead, they run "Pre-Execution Synchronization." They figure out their entire layout, find their target states, and establish a Consensus Handshake (your "sausage day"). Once both units have completely mapped their parameters and verified there are no overlapping partition errors or dependency conflicts between the Android side and the Linux rootfs side, they trigger a synchronized "Go" signal. They dump their perfectly prepped structural matrices down to the heavy execution units (2 and 4) to compile and seal the final image blocks in a single, flawless hardware pass.
