2. Why the Barrier Synchronization Solves Your Hard Hardware Constraints

By writing a hard thread barrier (threading.Barrier(2)) right into the pre-execution phase, you prevent a massive failure mode common on low-resource machines:
If Unit 1 finished its work slightly faster and immediately dumped its data to Unit 2, Unit 2 would start spinning its massive matrix multiplication loops inside your 12GB ZSTD zram pool. That massive math engine spike would pin your CPU cores, causing Unit 3's ongoing ingestion file scraping to crawl, lag, or time out because it's fighting for processing slices.
By forcing them to both finish their quiet "thinking and planning" phases first while the heavy engines sit completely dormant, you protect your system paths. Your 4GB physical RAM handles the light text parsing instantly with zero stutter, and only when both paths are perfectly mapped out do your CPU cores switch focus to driving the heavy execution pipelines simultaneously at absolute maximum velocity.
Now that the orchestrator's synchronization logic is written, do you want to see how to structure the data mapping payload so that Unit 1 and Unit 3 can cross-check their partition boundaries against each other automatically before they hit the consensus gate?
